<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tayma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tayma is an oasis town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> that was an important stop on ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. The Neo-Babylonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> resided there for around a decade in the sixth century BCE, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in the hands of his son. His absence prevented the New Year festival from being held, alienating the Babylonian priesthood before Cyrus's conquest.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tayma.jpg" alt="Tayma" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mortel · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_town_of_Tayma_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tayma, also spelled Tema, is a city and governorate in Tabuk Province, north-western Saudi Arabia, and a major oasis. It lies where the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and Dumah begins to cross the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafud">Nafud</a> desert, about 264 km south-east of Tabuk and 400 km north of Medina. It is one of the largest archaeological sites in Saudi Arabia, and its constant water supply made it a stopover even though it was not on the Frankincense Road.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest evidence of settled life is from the early 4th millennium BCE. In the mid-2nd millennium BCE the settlement expanded and was enclosed by a massive wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> and sandstone, and the domestication of the dromedary helped it become an economic centre. In the early Iron Age Egypt's influence is shown by the Pharaonic Tayma inscription, found in 2010 about 60 km to the north-west, the first hieroglyphic inscription found in Saudi Arabia, in the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a>. A building held Egyptian votive figurines and scarabs.</p><p>From the 8th century BCE Tayma had close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and was under Assyrian control without formal annexation. Assyrian governors raided caravans affiliated with it, and a gate inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> records gifts from the people of Sumu'il and Tema. Nabonidus, the last Babylonian king, used Tayma as the base for his campaigns in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, and inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> and Taymanitic name him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. After the Achaemenid empire fell, Tayma declined and came under the hegemony of the Lihyanite dynasty of Dedan; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it became a focus of the Nabataean Kingdom. Tayma appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, where Tema is a son of Ishmael and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> lists it among rich cities.</p><p>Arabic tradition describes a Jewish population in late antiquity, and pre-Islamic poetry associates Tayma with the poet Samaw'al ibn 'Adiya. Tradition says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were exiled under the caliph Umar. Medieval geographers praised its spring, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and palm groves, and it later became a rest stop on the pilgrimage route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the holy cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city walls described by al-Bakri are still visible and give the ruin its characteristic profile, and the oasis is known for its dates. Charles M. Doughty first mapped the site in 1877, and the Tayma stele found by Charles Huber in 1883, which lists the gods of Tayma in the 6th century BCE, is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Its monuments include Qasr Al-Ablaq, built in the 6th century by Samaw'al ibn 'Adiya and his grandfather 'Adiya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tayma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last king, Nabonidus, lacked dynastic legitimacy and added religious alienation to it: he favoured the moon god Sin over Marduk and spent roughly a decade at the Arabian oasis of <b>Tayma</b> while his son Belshazzar governed in Babylon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the mid-6th century BCE the Babylonian king Nabonidus campaigned in the Hejaz and listed Dedan among the oases he took while residing at <b>Tayma</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dedan">Kingdom of Dedan</a></li></ul></section>
